---
import Center from "../../components/Center.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="How to Generate Google Slides With Images From a Template">
  <Center>
    <div class="my-8">
      <div class="mb-1">
        <a href="/help" class="hover:underline">← Help</a>
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-8">
        How to Generate Google Slides With Images From a Template
      </h1>

      <figure class="mb-8">
        <iframe
          class="w-full aspect-video h-auto border border-gray-300 rounded-lg shadow-lg"
          width="560"
          height="315"
          src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/kd9Uq1imcms?si=CzhsuC5eAx9NXhKl"
          title="YouTube video player"
          frameborder="0"
          allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share"
          referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
          allowfullscreen></iframe>
        <figcaption class="text-center text-lg text-gray-700 mt-4 italic">
          Short demonstration video. Click to play!
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div
        class="prose prose-code:bg-gray-100 prose-code:border prose-code:border-gray-300 prose-code:px-1 prose-code:py-px prose-code:rounded prose-code:before:content-none prose-code:after:content-none"
      >
        <p>
          <a href="https://www.slidestencil.com/">Slide Stencil</a> is the Google
          Slides Add-on I built that helps you generate Google Slides from a template.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong
            >If you haven't used Slide Stencil before, <a
              href="/help/how-to-generate-google-slides-from-a-template"
              >this tutorial</a
            > will help you get started.</strong
          >
        </p>
        <p>
          Once you're familiar with the basics, you're ready to add images as
          well.
        </p>
        <h1 id="step-1-add-placeholder-images-to-your-template-slide">
          Step 1: Add Placeholder Images to Your Template Slide
        </h1>
        <p>
          The Slide Stencil sidebar includes a dedicated button called "+ Add
          Template Image" that helps you add placeholder images to your slide.
        </p>
        <p>
          Make sure you have Slide Stencil open and you've selected your
          template slide. (Follow <a
            href="/help/how-to-generate-google-slides-from-a-template"
            >this tutorial</a
          > if you haven't done this yet.)
        </p>
        <p>
          In the right sidebar, under the "Template Variables" header, find the
          "+ Add Template Image" button. When you click it, you will be prompted
          to choose a name. Just like with regular variables, the name can be
          anything you want. Whatever name you choose will be used as the column
          name in step 2.
        </p>
        <h1 id="step-2-insert-images-into-your-data-table">
          Step 2: Insert Images into your Data Table
        </h1>
        <p>
          In Shuffle Buddy, slides are generated from a data table. Each row of
          the table becomes one slide.
        </p>
        <p>We need a way to insert an image into a cell.</p>
        <div class="bg-yellow-100 border border-yellow-300 px-4 py-2 rounded">
          <p class="m-0!">
            <strong
              >If you are using Google Sheets, do NOT use the built-in "insert
              image into cell" functionality.</strong
            > I'm sorry; I know it's confusing! I can't make it work that way due
            to limitations in Google's Add-on platform.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p>
          Each cell can only contain text, so instead of pasting in an <em
            >image</em
          >, we're going to paste in the image's URL. That means you'll have to
          choose an image you find online (most likely on Google images) OR
          upload your image online yourself.
        </p>
        <p>To use an image from Google Images or any other website:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Find the image you want to use</li>
          <li>Right click on the image</li>
          <li>Choose "Copy Image Address" (NOT "Copy Image")</li>
          <li>Paste the image URL into the cell in your data table</li>
        </ol>
        <p>To use an image of your own:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            Upload the image to a website like <a href="https://cubeupload.com/"
              >CubeUpload</a
            >, <a href="https://imgbb.com/">ImgBB</a>, <a
              href="https://myimgs.org/">MyIMGs</a
            >, <a href="https://freeimage.host/">FreeImageHost</a>, or similar.
            (I am not endorsing any one service)
          </li>
          <li>
            Copy the image URL generated by the service. Remove anything that is
            not part of the link itself.
          </li>
          <li>Paste the image URL into the cell in your data table</li>
        </ol>
        <h1 id="step-3-generate-slides-with-template-images">
          Step 3: Generate Slides with Template Images
        </h1>
        <p>
          After completing steps 1-2, all you need to do is click the "Generate
          Slides" button.
        </p>
        <p>
          Your placeholder image from Step 1 will be replaced with the images
          you specified in your data table in Step 2.
        </p>
        <h1 id="i-make-more-apps-to-help-teachers-">
          I make more apps to help teachers!
        </h1>
        <p>
          If you like Shuffle Buddy, you might like <a
            href="https://www.joshuapullen.com/">the other apps I've made</a
          >. I am always building things to help teachers.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <a href="https://www.shufflebuddy.com/">Shuffle Buddy</a> for classroom
            seating charts
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://www.rocketspelling.com/">Rocket Spelling</a> for elementary
            spelling practice
          </li>
          <li>...And <a href="https://www.joshuapullen.com/">many more</a>!</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Best, Josh</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Center>
</Layout>
